# Real, conventional Intel-syntax x86-64 assembly for `impl.basm`'s instructions —
# assigned here, explicitly, rather than left to whichever ones happen to already
# read right under default positional `name arg, arg, ...` syntax, mirroring
# `std/riscv/native.basm`'s own stated convention (see its header comment) for the
# same reason: this file is then a complete, standalone spec of "native" syntax on
# its own.
#
# =================
# operand size: always 64-bit
# =================
# Every mnemonic below drops `impl.basm`'s explicit trailing `w: int` parameter and
# hardcodes `w = 1` (64-bit) — there is no 32-bit register name family in this
# package (`r0..r15`/`rax`/`rbx`/... are the *only* register spellings `impl.basm`
# defines; unlike real x86-64 assembly, there's no separate `eax`/`ebx`/... set to
# read a 32-bit operand size off of), so a 32-bit form has no natural spelling to
# give it here. 32-bit forms remain fully reachable — this file only *adds* syntax
# sugar, it never removes `impl.basm`'s own default positional syntax — by writing
# the `w` argument explicitly, e.g. `mov rax, rbx, 0`; every mnemonic below also
# gets an explicit "restatement" pattern for its own real, full-arity signature (see
# the next section) for exactly this reason.
#
# =================
# why almost every mnemonic needs *two* patterns, not one
# =================
# Once a name has *any* registered syntax, default positional syntax for that name
# is gone entirely — not just for the shape the new syntax covers, but for every
# other arity too (confirmed empirically: an early draft that dropped `w` via a
# reduced-arity wrapper, with no restatement alongside it, made every one of this
# file's *own* delegating calls to the real 3-argument `impl.basm` macro a parse
# error, since `mov`, once claimed, no longer accepts a bare `mov $a$, $b$, $c$`
# unless something says it still can). So for every mnemonic that gets a reduced,
# `w`-dropping pattern below, this file also registers a second pattern that just
# restates the real underlying macro's own full argument list verbatim
# (`syntax mov(a, b, c) = { mov $a$, $b$, $c$ }`) — not because it changes
# anything (it's byte-for-byte what default syntax already produced), but because
# it has to be there for *any* 3-argument `mov`/`add`/etc. call to keep parsing at
# all, including this file's own internal ones. A standalone restatement has to be
# declared *before* the reduced-arity macro it accompanies in this file — also
# confirmed empirically (declaring it after produced a spurious parse error on the
# very call it was meant to keep working) — so every section below follows that
# order.
#
# =================
# memory operands: real `[...]` syntax, and why it needs a compiler fix first
# =================
# `[base]`, `[base+disp]`, `[base+index*scale+disp]`, and `[rip+disp]` all *look*
# like they should be four independent patterns, but a `syntax` capture is an
# unbounded, generic expression parse, stopped only by the next literal token in
# its *own* pattern — so `[$base$+$disp$]`'s `disp` capture, bounded only by `]`,
# happily swallows an indexed form's entire `$index$*$scale$+$disp$` right-hand
# side as one ordinary, well-typed arithmetic expression (`rcx*4+0x10` type-checks
# fine as a plain `int`, so there's no type error for a later type-check to catch —
# both readings are equally well-typed, they just disagree on what the source text
# even *means*). The same trap catches `[base]` vs. `[base+disp]` (the empty-disp
# capture swallows `+disp` too) and `[base+disp]` vs. `[rip+disp]` (a bare
# identifier is itself a valid captured expression, so a generic `base` capture
# also matches literal `rip`). Confirmed empirically before this file was written:
# registering all four patterns made every one of them a hard "ambiguous syntax"
# parse error, including `PROGRESS.md`'s own flagship
# `mov rax, [rbx+rcx*4+0x10]` example.
#
# Fixed at the compiler level, not worked around here: `src/parser/
# invocation_syntax.rs`'s `parse_invocation_via_syntax_candidates` now prefers
# whichever successful match used the *most* literal (non-capture) tokens, when
# more than one candidate parses the same text — `[base+index*scale+disp]`'s 5
# literal tokens (`[`,`+`,`*`,`+`,`]`) beat `[base+disp]`'s 3 (`[`,`+`,`]`), which
# beat `[base]`'s 2 (`[`,`]`); `[rip+disp]`'s literal `rip` token gives it 4,
# beating `[base+disp]`'s 3. Two patterns that are *genuinely* equally specific
# (like `$a$ + $b$` vs. `$b$ + $a$`) are untouched by this and still hit the
# original ambiguity error. See that file's own comment, and the commit that added
# it, for the full reasoning and the new tests covering it.
#
# =================
# reg,reg vs. reg,imm: still separate mnemonics, not overloaded
# =================
# `movi`/`addi`/.../`testi` keep `impl.basm`'s own `i`-suffixed names rather than
# merging into `mov`/`add`/... the way real Intel syntax spells both forms
# identically: `Reg` is a plain `int` alias (`impl.basm`'s own Phase 1 note), so
# `mov(rd, rs)` and `mov(rd, imm)` would both resolve to identical `(int, int)`
# argument types once parsed — indistinguishable to the resolver regardless of
# which literal syntax pattern produced the call, since overload resolution runs
# on the parsed invocation's argument *types*, not on which pattern matched it.
# (Memory-operand forms don't have this problem — see the section above: a
# bracketed operand is lexically distinct from a bare one, so `mov`'s many memory
# shapes safely share its name without ever reaching this ambiguity.)

from .impl import *

# `Reg` is a plain `int` alias with no invariant of its own (`impl.basm`'s own
# Phase 1 note), so a reg,reg wrapper's second parameter accepts *any* `int`
# without complaint — including a bare immediate a caller meant for
# `movi`/`addi`/etc. instead. `mov rax, 100` parses cleanly as this file's own
# reg,reg `mov`, treating `100` as if it were register number 100 —
# `reg_field`/`reg_ext` then mask/shift it into nonsense bits instead of
# erroring (confirmed empirically while building Phase 7's oracle harness,
# where a shared `.s`/`.basm` test file can't spell an immediate `mov`/`add`/...
# at all — see `std/x86_64/PROGRESS.md`'s Phase 7 section). This doesn't catch
# every mistake (5 is also `rbp`'s own number, so `add rax, 5` meant as an
# immediate silently becomes `add rax, rbp` instead), but it does catch the
# common case of an out-of-range value, which a real immediate very often is.
# `pub`, empirically required: called only from this same file's own
# `| syntax {...}`-faceted macro bodies below, but a non-`pub` declaration's
# within-file references didn't resolve correctly once imported from elsewhere
# (`unknown macro` from a file that only ever imports this one) — not
# investigated further, `pub` costs nothing here.
pub macro assert_valid_reg(r: Reg) {
    @assert r >= 0 && r <= 15, "not a valid register (0-15) — use the `i`-suffixed mnemonic for an immediate"
}

# =================
# mov
# =================

syntax mov(a, b, c) = { mov $a$, $b$, $c$ }

pub macro mov(rd: Reg, rs: Reg) | syntax { mov $rd$, $rs$ } {
    assert_valid_reg rs
    mov rd, rs, 1
}

# --- load direction: reg <- [mem] ---

pub macro mov_load_base(rd: Reg, base: Reg) | syntax { mov $rd$, [$base$] } {
    mov rd, Mem(base, 0), 1
}

pub macro mov_load_base_disp(rd: Reg, base: Reg, disp: int) | syntax { mov $rd$, [$base$+$disp$] } {
    mov rd, Mem(base, disp), 1
}

pub macro mov_load_indexed(rd: Reg, base: Reg, index: Reg, scale: int, disp: int)
    | syntax { mov $rd$, [$base$+$index$*$scale$+$disp$] }
{
    mov rd, MemIndexed(base, index, scale, disp), 1
}

pub macro mov_load_rip(rd: Reg, disp: int) | syntax { mov $rd$, [rip+$disp$] } {
    mov rd, MemRipRelative(disp), 1
}

# --- store direction: [mem] <- reg ---

pub macro mov_store_base(base: Reg, rs: Reg) | syntax { mov [$base$], $rs$ } {
    mov Mem(base, 0), rs, 1
}

pub macro mov_store_base_disp(base: Reg, disp: int, rs: Reg) | syntax { mov [$base$+$disp$], $rs$ } {
    mov Mem(base, disp), rs, 1
}

pub macro mov_store_indexed(base: Reg, index: Reg, scale: int, disp: int, rs: Reg)
    | syntax { mov [$base$+$index$*$scale$+$disp$], $rs$ }
{
    mov MemIndexed(base, index, scale, disp), rs, 1
}

pub macro mov_store_rip(disp: int, rs: Reg) | syntax { mov [rip+$disp$], $rs$ } {
    mov MemRipRelative(disp), rs, 1
}

# =================
# ALU ops: add, or, and, sub, xor, cmp
# =================
# Register-only sugar (drop `w`, hardcode 64-bit) — memory-destination forms
# (`add [rbx+4], rax, 1`'s style bracket sugar) aren't built here: the exact same
# mechanical pattern `mov`'s store direction above demonstrates would apply
# unchanged, just not worth six more near-identical copies of it for this file's
# own sake. `impl.basm`'s own `Mem(...)`/`MemIndexed(...)`/`MemRipRelative(...)`
# constructor calls remain directly usable as an ALU op's second operand either
# way (already ordinary expressions, not needing any dialect support at all) —
# `add Mem(rax, 4), rbx, 1` still works verbatim with this file imported.

syntax add(a, b, c) = { add $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro add(rd: Reg, rs: Reg) | syntax { add $rd$, $rs$ } {
    assert_valid_reg rs
    add rd, rs, 1
}

syntax or(a, b, c) = { or $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro or(rd: Reg, rs: Reg) | syntax { or $rd$, $rs$ } {
    assert_valid_reg rs
    or rd, rs, 1
}

syntax and(a, b, c) = { and $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro and(rd: Reg, rs: Reg) | syntax { and $rd$, $rs$ } {
    assert_valid_reg rs
    and rd, rs, 1
}

syntax sub(a, b, c) = { sub $a$, $b$, $c$ }

# Named `sub2`, not `sub`: a real `sub(rd: Reg, rs: Reg)` (both `Reg` = `int`)
# would resolve to `(int, int)` — identical to `std.bitter.deferred`'s own
# `sub(a: int, b: int)`, always in scope (see this file's own header comment
# on why naming an import doesn't limit what's transitively spliced in,
# `std/x86_64/impl.basm`'s Phase 5 note on `shl_cl`/`shr_cl`/`sar_cl` first
# ran into this for the exact same reason). Unanchored (its pattern's own
# leading token is the real mnemonic `sub`, not `sub2`), so `sub2` itself is
# never "claimed" or user-facing at all.
pub macro sub2(rd: Reg, rs: Reg) | syntax { sub $rd$, $rs$ } {
    assert_valid_reg rs
    sub rd, rs, 1
}

syntax xor(a, b, c) = { xor $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro xor(rd: Reg, rs: Reg) | syntax { xor $rd$, $rs$ } {
    assert_valid_reg rs
    xor rd, rs, 1
}

syntax cmp(a, b, c) = { cmp $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro cmp(rd: Reg, rs: Reg) | syntax { cmp $rd$, $rs$ } {
    assert_valid_reg rs
    cmp rd, rs, 1
}

syntax test(a, b, c) = { test $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro test(rd: Reg, rs: Reg) | syntax { test $rd$, $rs$ } {
    assert_valid_reg rs
    test rd, rs, 1
}

# reg,imm forms — same treatment, `impl.basm`'s own `i`-suffixed names kept (see
# this file's own header comment on why).

syntax movi(a, b, c) = { movi $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro movi(rd: Reg, imm: int) | syntax { movi $rd$, $imm$ } { movi rd, imm, 1 }

syntax addi(a, b, c) = { addi $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro addi(rd: Reg, imm: int) | syntax { addi $rd$, $imm$ } { addi rd, imm, 1 }

syntax ori(a, b, c) = { ori $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro ori(rd: Reg, imm: int) | syntax { ori $rd$, $imm$ } { ori rd, imm, 1 }

syntax andi(a, b, c) = { andi $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro andi(rd: Reg, imm: int) | syntax { andi $rd$, $imm$ } { andi rd, imm, 1 }

syntax subi(a, b, c) = { subi $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro subi(rd: Reg, imm: int) | syntax { subi $rd$, $imm$ } { subi rd, imm, 1 }

syntax xori(a, b, c) = { xori $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro xori(rd: Reg, imm: int) | syntax { xori $rd$, $imm$ } { xori rd, imm, 1 }

syntax cmpi(a, b, c) = { cmpi $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro cmpi(rd: Reg, imm: int) | syntax { cmpi $rd$, $imm$ } { cmpi rd, imm, 1 }

syntax testi(a, b, c) = { testi $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro testi(rd: Reg, imm: int) | syntax { testi $rd$, $imm$ } { testi rd, imm, 1 }

# =================
# lea
# =================
# Address-only — reuses the exact same four addressing-mode patterns `mov`'s load
# direction above establishes, since `lea`'s own encoding shape (Phase 5) is
# identical to a memory *load*'s (reg field is the destination, `MemOperand` the
# address), just a different opcode.

pub macro lea_base(rd: Reg, base: Reg) | syntax { lea $rd$, [$base$] } {
    lea rd, Mem(base, 0), 1
}

pub macro lea_base_disp(rd: Reg, base: Reg, disp: int) | syntax { lea $rd$, [$base$+$disp$] } {
    lea rd, Mem(base, disp), 1
}

pub macro lea_indexed(rd: Reg, base: Reg, index: Reg, scale: int, disp: int)
    | syntax { lea $rd$, [$base$+$index$*$scale$+$disp$] }
{
    lea rd, MemIndexed(base, index, scale, disp), 1
}

pub macro lea_rip(rd: Reg, disp: int) | syntax { lea $rd$, [rip+$disp$] } {
    lea rd, MemRipRelative(disp), 1
}

# =================
# shl/shr/sar
# =================
# reg,imm8 gets the same drop-`w` treatment as the ALU ops. The reg,CL form gets
# genuine `$rd$, cl` syntax this time (Phase 5's own notes explain why it didn't
# get this originally: a literal `cl` token and a generic `$imm$` capture both
# successfully parse `shl rax, cl`, which used to be an unconditional "ambiguous
# syntax" error before this file's own literal-specificity compiler fix — the
# literal `cl` pattern's extra literal token now correctly wins). Registered as an
# *unanchored* pattern on `shl_cl`/`shr_cl`/`sar_cl` themselves (their own real
# names, never spelled by a caller) rather than adding a third overload of
# `shl`/`shr`/`sar` — since the pattern's own leading token is the real mnemonic
# (`shl`, not `shl_cl`), `shl_cl` itself is never "claimed" and needs no
# restatement of its own.

syntax shl(a, b, c) = { shl $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro shl(rd: Reg, imm: int) | syntax { shl $rd$, $imm$ } { shl rd, imm, 1 }
pub macro shl_cl(rd: Reg) | syntax { shl $rd$, cl } { shl_cl rd, 1 }

syntax shr(a, b, c) = { shr $a$, $b$, $c$ }

# Named `shr_imm`, not `shr` — the exact same `std.bitter.deferred`-collision
# reason as `sub2` above (`shr(rd: Reg, imm: int)` would be `(int, int)`,
# identical to `std.bitter.deferred`'s own `shr(a: int, b: int)`).
pub macro shr_imm(rd: Reg, imm: int) | syntax { shr $rd$, $imm$ } { shr rd, imm, 1 }
pub macro shr_cl(rd: Reg) | syntax { shr $rd$, cl } { shr_cl rd, 1 }

syntax sar(a, b, c) = { sar $a$, $b$, $c$ }
pub macro sar(rd: Reg, imm: int) | syntax { sar $rd$, $imm$ } { sar rd, imm, 1 }
pub macro sar_cl(rd: Reg) | syntax { sar $rd$, cl } { sar_cl rd, 1 }

# =================
# push/pop, control flow, ret
# =================
# None of these have a `w` parameter in `impl.basm` at all (Phase 4/5's own
# notes explain why — `push`/`pop` have no 32-bit form in 64-bit mode, and
# `jmp`/`jcc`/`call`/`ret` have no operand-size concept whatsoever), so nothing
# here is "claimed" by anything else and none of these strictly *need* a
# restatement for some other shape to keep working. Restated explicitly anyway,
# matching `std/riscv/native.basm`'s own stated convention of giving every
# mnemonic its own explicit entry regardless of whether default syntax would
# already suffice.

syntax push(rd) = { push $rd$ }
syntax pop(rd) = { pop $rd$ }

syntax jmp(target) = { jmp $target$ }
syntax call(target) = { call $target$ }
syntax ret() = { ret }

syntax je(target) = { je $target$ }
syntax jne(target) = { jne $target$ }
syntax jb(target) = { jb $target$ }
syntax jae(target) = { jae $target$ }
syntax ja(target) = { ja $target$ }
syntax jbe(target) = { jbe $target$ }
syntax jl(target) = { jl $target$ }
syntax jge(target) = { jge $target$ }
syntax jle(target) = { jle $target$ }
syntax jg(target) = { jg $target$ }
syntax js(target) = { js $target$ }
syntax jns(target) = { jns $target$ }
syntax jo(target) = { jo $target$ }
syntax jno(target) = { jno $target$ }
syntax jp(target) = { jp $target$ }
syntax jnp(target) = { jnp $target$ }
